fact WorkItem(id: "WI-835", description: some(value: "Enforce a parametric sort's `requires Eq[param]` at TYPE-INSTANTIATION sites: `Map[K = Float]` / `Set[T = Float]` must FAIL to load, while `Map[K = TotalFloat]` keeps loading. This is WI-644's ONE outstanding acceptance item (library proposal 004).\n\nWHY THIS EXISTS -- ORPHANED ENFORCEMENT (the second time on this same feature). WI-644's ACCEPTANCE requires \"Map[K=Float] is a load error, Map[K=TotalFloat] loads\". Its feedback (2026-07-08) CONFIRMED that does not hold, gave the mechanism, and deferred it to a focused session with \"Not a spinoff -- this is WI-644's own acceptance\"; WI-644 was then marked Delivered, so the item appears in NO open-work list. WI-658's description points at \"sibling WI-649 (use-site requires Eq[K] => Map[K=Float] load error)\" -- but WI-649 is the reify/reify_value cyclic-sigma occurs-check ticket (Delivered), i.e. that number was REPURPOSED. This is exactly the failure WI-658 was itself created to fix, when the provision-site half had been mis-attributed to the repurposed WI-650. Net: the use-site half has had no owner since 2026-07-08. WI-658's stale \"sibling WI-649\" pointer should be repointed HERE.\n\nEMPIRICAL GAP (verified 2026-07-28, `anthill load` with the embedded stdlib): a program declaring `operation m() -> Map[K = Float, V = Int64] = Map.empty()` loads CLEAN; the `Map[K = TotalFloat, V = Int64]` control ALSO loads clean. So the acceptance pair currently passes only VACUOUSLY -- nothing distinguishes the two spellings, and `TotalFloat` is a documentation convention rather than an enforced boundary. ROOT CAUSE per WI-644's feedback: WI-616 made `requires Eq` a vacuous universal-structural default, so the sort-level `requires Eq[T = K]` (stdlib/anthill/prelude/map.anthill:7) and `requires Eq[T]` (set.anthill:10) constrain nothing at instantiation.\n\nMECHANISM (from WI-644's own feedback; NO new language surface): at a TYPE-INSTANTIATION site, for each binding that a parametric sort's `requires Spec[param]` ranges over, check that the bound carrier PROVIDES the spec (`sort_provides`). `Float` provides PartialEq / PartialOrd / NonEq but NOT `Eq` (float.anthill), so `Map[K = Float]` is refused with a diagnostic naming the sort, the parameter, the carrier, the required spec, and the lawful alternative (`TotalFloat`). `TotalFloat` provides `Eq` (totalfloat.anthill:33-34), so it is accepted. This COMPLEMENTS WI-658 (delivered): provision-site law refutation there, use-site provision check here -- the two halves of library/004's lawful-key guarantee.\n\nRISK / REQUIRED AUDIT (why WI-644 deferred it rather than tacking it on): the check is vacuous today, so giving it teeth can REJECT programs that load now -- every `Map` / `Set` / dedup / sorted-key instantiation over a carrier that does not provide lawful `Eq`, including entity carriers that WI-664's composite derivation classifies `NonEq` (a plain entity with a `Float` field). AUDIT stdlib + rustland tests + examples/ + anthill-todo/ before landing, and expect to weaken some `requires Eq` to `requires PartialEq` where only comparison is used (WI-644's MIGRATION note).\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n - `Map[K = Float, V = _]` and `Set[T = Float]` FAIL to load, with a diagnostic naming the sort, the parameter, the carrier and the required spec.\n - `Map[K = TotalFloat]`, `Map[K = Int64]`, `Map[K = String]`, `Set[T = Int64]` still load.\n - A `Map` keyed by an entity carrier that WI-664 classifies `NonEq` (an entity with a `Float` field) FAILS to load; a lawful entity key still loads.\n - The refusal is a LOAD error, never deferred to eval (loud-over-silent).\n - Full workspace `cargo-test` green AFTER the audit.\n - WI-644's last acceptance item is then met, and library/004's Status can move off Draft (this is its only unshipped bullet -- the PartialEq/Eq/PartialOrd/Ord split, NonEq, and TotalFloat all shipped).\n\nREF: docs/proposals/library/004-partial-vs-total-equality-and-ordering.md; WI-644 (parent -- this IS its outstanding acceptance, and the parent now depends on this); WI-658 (provision-site sibling, delivered -- its \"sibling WI-649\" pointer is stale and means THIS ticket); WI-664 (composite Eq/NonEq derivation, delivered); WI-616 (made `requires Eq` vacuous); WI-649 (NOT this -- repurposed to the reify cyclic-sigma occurs-check; do not rely on that number); stdlib/anthill/prelude/{map,set,eq,float,totalfloat}.anthill; rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs (type-instantiation checking); docs/proposals/058-modular-instances.md §6 (records this gap and the measurement). Rust + scaland (scaland mirrors only if it gains provides-checking)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-29T14:35:59Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-835", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-07-29 (commit c1c80110). The ticket's premise was half wrong and driving it corrected two things. (1) The check was NOT missing: check_use_site_requires_eq shipped with WI-644 and was already NEGATIVE. The ticket's prescribed MECHANISM (a positive sort_provides(carrier, Eq) test) was NOT built -- the existing doc argues in detail why positive is both vacuous (WI-616's universal structural default) and over-rejecting (WI-664 derives NonEq for partial composites but no Eq for lawful ones), and driving confirmed it. The defect was purely the check's SCOPE: entity FIELD types only, so the ticket's own acceptance program (an operation RETURN type) was invisible. (2) The RISK/AUDIT paragraph predicted widening 'can REJECT programs that load now ... expect to weaken some requires Eq to requires PartialEq'. MEASURED: zero fallout. stdlib, examples/, anthill-todo/, anthill-testcases/, cpp-gen and cli fixtures all load clean, and the full suite was green (87 binaries, 3678 passed) with no stdlib weakening at all. FIX SHAPE: the position list is gone -- type_expr_to_child's Parameterized arm (the sole type lowering, already the home of WI-709's syntactic arg-fit check) records every written instantiation; the check walks that after eq_derive::run. Nine positions measured covered and pinned individually, three beyond the acceptance: a sort alias, a body let annotation, a typed lambda binder, and nesting inside a tuple / arrow / another instantiation (Map[K = Set[T = Float]] now refuses on the INNER Set at its own span -- WI-644's doc had listed that as deliberate non-scope). My own draft doc claimed body annotations were out of scope; a probe refuted it before it shipped. DIAGNOSTIC: names sort, the CONTAINER's parameter (K, not Eq's T -- recovered via a second empty-sigma requires walk, since reverse-lookup by carrier misnames it for Map[K = Float, V = Float]), the carrier and the required spec, and is LOCATED path:line:col. TWO SILENT DROPS found reviewing my own new code: the per-site dedup key carried byte offsets without their SourceId, so two byte-identical-layout files reported ONE refusal instead of two (measured 1 vs 2, now pinned); and Display omitted the span that dedup_key is built from. PERF: load time unchanged (0.62-0.67s vs 0.67-0.91s stashed baseline), so the per-site requires walk was left unmemoized despite being redundant across duplicate types. DOCS: library/004 Status moved off Draft (its two Open questions stay open, incl. TotalFloat not being Ordered); 058 section 6's 'not enforced today' updated; kernel-language.md section on equality gained the refusal rule and lost a false claim that TotalFloat provides Ordered; float.anthill's and WI-658's stale 'WI-649' pointers repointed here; the map.anthill:7 / set.anthill:10 line citations my stdlib comments would have invalidated were dropped. NOT MIRRORED to scaland -- it has no SortProvidesInfo/sort_provides at all and parameterized requires is a literal TODO in load/Loader.scala, which is the ticket's own stated condition. CAVEAT: /code-review was NOT run (not available as a skill in this session); I ran the four /simplify angles myself, which produced the type_expr_span reuse, the located_in_kb_source extraction, and both silent-drop findings above.", at: "2026-07-29T14:36:26Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-835", author: "user", content: "/code-review ROUND (commit 07a69e5f, after the c1c80110 delivery). The review returned 12 findings; I drove each rather than accepting them, and one of my OWN first probes was invalid (I used a nonexistent 'Vector' sort, so the file died on unresolved names and my grep read that as 'loads clean'). Two findings were REAL HOLES, both from ONE decision -- recording the assembled type TERM instead of the bindings the lowering had already resolved. (1) A DENOTED sibling binding switched the check OFF for the carriers beside it: Map[K = Float, V = Buf[T = Int64, N = 3]] loaded CLEAN while the identical type without the literal was refused (measured, plus a lawful-key control proving the literal itself was not the cause). The value-in-type poisons the whole instantiation to Value::Node, which has no ground term, so K = Float was dropped with it -- adding an unrelated argument disabled a soundness check. My comment had justified skipping the Node branch as 'those bind a VALUE, not a carrier', which is true of the denoted binding and false of its siblings; I never probed a MIXED instantiation. (2) 'The sole type lowering' is TWO: sort_binding_to_value has its own TypeExpr::Parameterized arm, so a container written in a requires/provides clause was unrecorded (requires Iterable[C = Map[K = Float]] loaded clean). I had READ that function and still wrote 'sole', quoting type_expr_to_value's own wrong doc. FIX: the registry now stores ParameterizedSite { base, bindings, span } -- a denoted binding drops ITSELF, never the site -- and both lowerings record. That also deleted a DEAD positional branch in the check (the lowering maps positionals to declared names before assembling, so a recorded site is always named) which a new test's docstring falsely claimed to pin. Further review fixes: the registry is DRAINED (push-only meant a second load_incremental re-walked and re-reported every earlier batch); an empty-bindings site is not recorded; the per-base Eq-clause walk is cached (direct_requires_chain clones its memoized vector per call, and the check now visits every written Option[..]/List[..]); the bare-name decode got one owner shared with the substitution that must agree with it; located_in_source's doc comment -- which c1c80110 accidentally reassigned to a new function -- is back; and the Display byte-range comment no longer claims a dedup path these errors do not take. RESTORED a gap my doc rewrite had DELETED: a key unlawful in its ARGUMENT (Map[K = List[T = Float]], Map[K = (a: Float)]) is still not caught, since the check reads the key's own provisions and neither carrier provides NonEq; both are genuinely unlawful keys, so it is a real gap owned by WI-664's propagation follow-up, now pinned by a test named known_gap_ so it cannot quietly stop being documented. Audit re-run clean; full suite 87 binaries / 3682 passed / 0 failed; load time unchanged (interleaved A/B against a pre-WI-835 binary, minimum 0.62s both, spread is machine noise). Findings NOT acted on: none material -- the remaining ones were the same items in different words.", at: "2026-07-29T15:20:58Z")
